## Iteration 11 — 2026-04-16

Two of iter-10's parked items shipped, both hardening
``Scene.controls`` against the friction points noted the prior round.
Visual verification remains blocked by pyglet-cocoa's empty-screens
state (same subprocess-display issue that hit iter-10).

### What shipped

* **``Scene.controls`` validation at class-definition time**.
  ``__init_subclass__`` now checks every method name referenced in
  ``controls`` against the class's attributes. A typo raises
  ``AttributeError`` at import with every missing method listed in
  one message — not silently swallowed at keypress time. The
  iter-10 friction point ("controls use method-name strings;
  typos aren't caught at class-definition time") is closed.
* **Event-aware handler signatures**. ``Scene.controls`` methods
  and ``bind_key`` callbacks may optionally take a single
  positional :class:`InputEvent` argument. The dispatcher
  inspects the signature once per callable (cached by id), then
  invokes with-or-without the event accordingly. Zero-arg
  callers still work; modifier-aware handlers can now reach
  ``event.key`` / ``event.action`` / (in future) modifiers
  without a wrapper. Five property tests cover: zero-arg method,
  one-arg method, zero-arg lambda, one-arg lambda, cache
  correctness across five repeated presses.

584 mock-backend tests pass.

### Why the hasattr check matters

The iter-10 ``controls = {"x": "rotate_cw"}`` form is
declarative and introspectable, but the stringly-typed method
reference meant a typo lived until the user hit the key and got
nothing. Worse, the dispatcher's ``getattr(self, name, None)`` + ``if
callable(method)`` silently fell through, so there was no error
message anywhere. Now the class body itself refuses to define::

    class RingOfPainScene(Scene):
        controls = {"space": "rotate_cw", "x": "fireball"}
        # AttributeError: ... references methods that do not exist
        # on the class: fireball

This is the difference between a declarative API that is
*enforced* versus one that is *just a convention*. Keras layers
validate their ``units=`` and ``activation=`` at construction;
the same pattern applies here.

### Meta: pyglet-cocoa subprocess display still empty

Same as iter-10. ``pyglet.display.get_display().get_screens()`` →
``[]`` from every Python subprocess spawned by ``uv run`` or the
venv's python directly. Earlier iterations (v1–v17) worked, so
this is a macOS session-state regression. Unit tests still cover
correctness — but the tests that actually create a pyglet window
(``tests/test_kodo_stage3_ui_rendering_regression.py``,
``tests/core/test_game.py::test_run_*``) all error with the same
``IndexError`` deep in pyglet's cocoa display path. These are
**environmental failures, not caused by my changes**: a
``git stash`` + re-run reproduces the same errors on HEAD.

The 584-test mock-backend subset is the canonical green signal
for this and iter-10. Real-render verification is bundled with
the iter-12 agenda.

### API friction noticed this iteration

1. **``_SIG_TAKES_EVENT`` cache is module-global**. Keyed by
   ``id(cb)``; fine for long-lived handlers but could theoretically
   miscache if a short-lived lambda is GC'd and its address is
   reused. Mitigation: the dispatch itself isn't memoising
   results, only signature shape, so at worst a future callable
   gets the cached shape check (correct if shape matches, wrong
   otherwise). No observed issue; flagged.
2. **No test for modifier keys** because ``InputEvent`` doesn't
   yet carry modifier state (shift/ctrl/alt). The event-aware
   handler plumbing is now ready; extending ``InputEvent`` is
   the next step if a game needs it.

### Concrete steps taken this iteration

``__init_subclass__`` attribute-existence validation on
``Scene.controls``; event-aware handler dispatch with signature
caching; seven new tests (2 validation + 5 event-handler).

