## Iteration 13 — 2026-04-16

Shipped the iter-12-parked "second example game" as a generalisation
check on the entire iter-2..12 API stack. Visual verification remains
blocked (fourth iteration on that blocker).

### What shipped

* **``examples/dial_menu/``** — a radial menu selector (6 options
  in a ring, arrow keys rotate, Enter confirms, Esc cancels).
  97 lines total including docstring and entry point. Exercises:
  - ``ring_positions`` + ``ring_budget`` for layout (no magic
    literals, same pattern as Ring of Pain).
  - ``Scene.controls`` class-level dict with tuple aliases for
    arrows + WASD + confirm + space.
  - Reactive ``Label`` bound to ``self.options[self.selected]``
    and ``self.status``.
  - ``Game(theme=build_theme())`` with three named text styles
    (``title``, ``heading``, ``caption``).
  - ``self.draw_circle`` / ``self.draw_text`` primitives.
  - ``Anchor.TOP_LEFT`` / ``CENTER`` / ``BOTTOM`` anchored UI.
* **Seven property tests** for the dial menu (initial state, CW/CCW
  rotation, left-wrap, confirm-updates-status, cancel-updates-status,
  custom-options-length, class-def-controls-validation). Every
  saga2d iter-2..12 API used by the dial menu is exercised.

675 mock-backend tests pass.

### What the generalisation check validates

Before this iteration, *every* API decision in iter-2..12 was
weighed against a single game (Ring of Pain). The risk was that
primitives had unintentionally specialised for that game's shape.

The dial menu is a different-shape game (selector, not roguelike):
- no combat, no state mutation per interaction
- fewer node types (one — a menu item)
- no HP/coin HUD
- simpler input map (4 actions, not 3 actions)
- needs 6 items on a ring, not 8

It composes cleanly from the same primitives. In particular:
- ``ring_budget`` was called with different margins (60/60 vs
  60/88) and produced a sensibly sized ring without retuning
  magic constants.
- ``Scene.controls`` accepted ``"cancel"`` as a single-string key
  alongside tuple-keyed ``("right", "d")`` — both syntaxes work.
- The reactive Label pattern ``Label(lambda: self.options[self.selected])``
  landed without needing new API.

This is the kind of test that's easy to forget to run in a
single-example project — the selected primitives "feel right"
because they fit the only game you're building. A second game of
a different shape is a cheap, high-signal generalisation check.

### Meta: pyglet display still empty

Fourth iteration unable to render. Tried ``uv run``, venv python
