## Iteration 17 — 2026-04-16

Completed the iter-11 → iter-16 event-dispatch arc: ``InputEvent``
now carries modifier state, the pyglet backend populates it from the
keypress bitmask, and event-aware handlers read modifiers end-to-end.
The feature round-trips through both backends and lands in a
tic-tac-toe shortcut.

### What shipped

* **``InputEvent.shift / ctrl / alt / meta``** — four new boolean
  fields on the ``InputEvent`` dataclass (default ``False``; keeps
  every existing test fixture working unchanged).
* **``KeyEvent`` grew the same four fields**. The backend-to-framework
  event type mirrors the public one.
* **Pyglet backend wiring**. Added ``_mods_to_kwargs(modifiers)``
  that decodes pyglet's ``MOD_SHIFT`` / ``MOD_CTRL`` /
  ``MOD_ALT | MOD_OPTION`` / ``MOD_COMMAND | MOD_WINDOWS`` bitmask
  into the four booleans. The on_key_press and on_key_release
  handlers now pass modifier state through to the KeyEvent.
  Cross-platform: ``alt`` fires on Option (macOS) or Alt (Win);
  ``meta`` fires on Cmd (macOS) or Windows key.
* **Mock backend ``inject_key(..., shift=True, ctrl=False, ...)``**
  kwargs. Tests can simulate any modifier combination without going
  through pyglet. Five property tests cover: defaults-false,
  shift-flows-through, meta+ctrl combo, event-aware Scene handler
  reads ``event.shift``, bind_key lambda sees modifiers.
* **``InputManager.translate``** passes the four booleans through
  from KeyEvent to InputEvent.
* **Tic-tac-toe demo**: ``Shift+R`` opens with X in the centre, plain
  ``R`` is a full reset. The ``reset`` method declares
  ``def reset(self, event: InputEvent | None = None)`` — iter-11's
  signature-aware dispatcher passes the event when the method takes
  one arg, omits it otherwise. No code at the call site; the scene
  just reads ``event.shift`` inside the handler. New test covers
  the shortcut.

709 mock-backend tests pass.

### Why this arc matters

Three iterations of plumbing (iter-11 signature-aware dispatch,
iter-16 cache removal, iter-17 modifier fields) converge into one
game-level feature: a declarative shortcut modifier. The arc:

* iter-11 added the ``cb`` takes-event detection so handlers could
  be zero-arg or one-arg without a wrapper.
* iter-16 removed the half-baked cache that made that detection
  inefficient-and-unsafe.
* iter-17 populated the event with the actual modifier state.

Now a scene can write::

    class MyScene(Scene):
        controls = {"r": "reset"}
        def reset(self, event):
            if event.shift: self._reset_to_checkpoint()
            else: self._full_reset()

— and saga2d's dispatcher handles everything: the declarative
class-level mapping, the method-name validation at class-def time,
the signature-aware invocation, the modifier state propagation.
What the game sees is ``if event.shift``. What the framework
handles is an entire dispatch chain that didn't exist as one piece
before.

### Meta: eight iterations unrendered

Still pyglet-cocoa empty screens from a subprocess. Tally:

| iter | shipped                                                  |
|------|---------------------------------------------------------|
|  10  | ``Scene.controls`` class-level declarative input        |
|  11  | hasattr-validation + event-aware handler signatures     |
|  12  | ``ring_budget`` + ``ReactiveValue`` seed-from-callable  |
|  13  | dial-menu (second example, radial)                      |
|  14  | ``Selector`` + Anchor aliases                           |
|  15  | tic-tac-toe (third example, non-radial) + shim deletion |
|  16  | ``draw_rect`` border kwargs + cache removal             |
|  17  | InputEvent modifier fields (end-to-end modifier arc)    |

Mock-test count 566 → 709. Every feature added in this run is
exercised through a test against the mock backend, so the
no-render discipline has continued without sacrificing
correctness signal.

### API friction noticed this iteration

1. **``meta``'s cross-platform semantics leak into game code**.
   A scene that binds Cmd+Z on macOS and Ctrl+Z on Windows still
   has to write two different controls. saga2d could normalise
   this further — e.g. a synthetic ``"primary"`` modifier that's
   Cmd on macOS, Ctrl elsewhere — but overloading meta itself
   would surprise macOS users who want genuine Cmd-but-not-Ctrl
   behaviour. Not urgent.
2. **``KeyEvent`` modifier booleans default ``False`` for
   backward compat** — fine, but means a backend that *doesn't*
   populate them is indistinguishable from a user holding no
   modifiers. No observable bug today; flagged.

### Concrete steps taken this iteration

Four dataclass fields, one 20-line helper, one InputManager
pass-through, one mock-backend ``inject_key`` kwargs addition, one
tic-tac-toe demo, five new tests. The "plumbing already ready"
promise from iter-11 kept.

