## Iteration 19 — 2026-04-17

Closed the iter-18 friction point on the second try: tests no longer
need to reach into ``_children``. One pure addition (walk / find /
find_all) plus migration of the iter-18 README tests.

### What shipped

* **``Component.walk(include_self=False)``** — depth-first
  generator over descendants. Yields each component in
  painter-draw order so tests and tooling can see the whole
  tree without touching private attributes.
* **``Component.find(predicate, include_self=False)``** — returns
  the first descendant matching *predicate*, or ``None``. Shortcut
  for the idiomatic ``next((c for c in walk() if p(c)), None)``.
* **``Component.find_all(predicate, include_self=False)``** —
  every matching descendant as a list.
* **iter-18 README tests migrated** from ``scene.ui._children``
  poking to ``scene.ui.find(lambda c: isinstance(c, Label) and
  c.text.startswith("HP"))``. Same assertions, public surface.
* **Eight property tests** on walk / find / find_all: DFS order,
  include_self semantics, first-match for find, None-on-miss,
  empty-on-miss for find_all, bare-component walk yields nothing
  (or just self with include_self=True).

723 mock-backend tests pass (8 new).

### Why this matters beyond test hygiene

Keras's ``Model.summary()`` and ``Model.layers`` are the introspection
surface that makes the framework debuggable. You print the model
and see every layer, trainable status, parameter count. You iterate
layers to attach custom hooks. That introspection is what makes it
possible for third-party tooling (TensorBoard, model visualisers)
to exist without Keras having to ship them.

``Component.walk`` is the same primitive for saga2d UI trees. A
debug HUD could walk a scene, print every component's class and
computed rect, draw its bounding box for layout debugging. A
snapshot tester could walk the tree and diff against a prior
snapshot. A stylesheet inspector could find every Label with a
given ``text_style`` and show what the theme resolves to. None of
these tools exist yet, but all of them are now *possible* via the
public API — that's what shipping introspection does.

