## Iteration 20 — 2026-04-17

Milestone round: shipped the most direct Keras-parallel to land in
saga2d yet — ``scene.summary()``. Keras's ``Model.summary()`` is
arguably the single feature most cited when people explain "why I
reach for Keras over raw TensorFlow" — given a model, tell me what's
in it. ``Scene.summary()`` is literally that for game scenes.

### What shipped

* **``Scene.summary() -> str``** returns a human-readable dump of
  a scene's declared structure:

    - scene class name
    - background colour if set
    - ``controls`` dict, grouped by target method (``right, d  →
      rotate_cw`` on one line instead of two)
    - UI tree via iter-19's ``walk``, indented by depth, each
      component shown with class + text (literal or ``← callable``)
      + text_style + anchor + margin
* **Two internal helpers**: ``_walk_with_depth`` (depth-pair
  generator) and ``_describe_component`` (one-line component
  description). Both are module-private — the formatting logic
  intentionally does not leak into the public surface.
* **Twelve property tests** covering every shape of output:
  scene class name present, background colour shown/hidden
  appropriately, aliased controls grouped on one line, static
  text shown literally vs. reactive shown as ``← callable``,
  text_style tag present when set, anchor + margin shown,
  ProgressBar with callable value flagged, nested children
  indented one level deeper than parent, empty scene renders
  without crashing, multi-line string returned.

735 mock-backend tests pass (12 new).

### Sample outputs (real, from the three examples)

Ring of Pain::

    Scene: RingOfPainScene
      background_color: (18, 14, 28, 255)
      controls:
        confirm, space  → interact
        a, left  → rotate_ccw
        d, right  → rotate_cw
      ui:
        Label "Ring of Pain", text_style=title, anchor=TOP_LEFT, margin=20
        Label ← callable, text_style=hud, anchor=TOP_RIGHT, margin=20
        Row anchor=BOTTOM_LEFT, margin=16
          Label ← callable, text_style=hud
          ProgressBar ← callable
          Label ← callable, text_style=hud
        Label "←  →  move     space  interact", text_style=caption, anchor=BOTTOM_RIGHT, margin=20
        Label ← callable, text_style=caption, anchor=BOTTOM, margin=48

    Scene: DialMenuScene
      background_color: (16, 18, 28, 255)
      controls:
        cancel  → cancel
        confirm, space  → confirm
        a, left  → rotate_ccw
        d, right  → rotate_cw
      ui:
        Label "Dial Menu", text_style=title, anchor=TOP_LEFT, margin=20
        Label ← callable, text_style=heading, anchor=CENTER
        Label ← callable, text_style=caption, anchor=BOTTOM, margin=24

Every declarative feature the scene uses shows up in the summary:
iter-10's ``controls`` dict, iter-4's reactive Labels (the ``←
callable`` marker), iter-5's ``text_style`` on Labels, iter-14's
Anchor vocabulary, iter-7's reactive ProgressBar.

### Why this is the capstone for the "Keras for game dev" positioning

Each earlier iteration added a declarative API surface. Until this
iteration, nothing told the developer *from a running scene* what
declarative wiring was actually in effect. The summary is the
readout that makes the rest of the stack *inspectable at runtime*:

* Did the ``controls`` dict validate? ``summary()`` shows the
  flattened keys; if a key is missing, you know immediately.
* Is a Label reactive, or did somebody type ``text="…"`` by
  mistake? ``← callable`` vs. ``"literal"`` in the output.
* Is the ``text_style`` hooked up? The tag appears in the
  description.
* Is a child inside the right parent? Indentation encodes the
  tree.

Keras's ``Model.summary`` plays this role for ML models. Scene
summary is the game-dev equivalent — same debugging loop, same
paste-into-a-bug-report value.

### Meta: eleven iterations unrendered

The no-render tally is now eleven consecutive iterations:

| iter | shipped                                                  |
|------|---------------------------------------------------------|
|  10  | ``Scene.controls`` class-level declarative input        |
|  11  | hasattr-validation + event-aware handler signatures     |
